<h3>Making something else in the look</h3>
<p>For anything that is not a web page — a thumbnail, a video, a carousel, a deck — the server hands over the look itself, so a person or an assistant can make it in any tool: Final Cut, Premiere, DaVinci, Keynote, Figma, Canva, an image model.</p>
<div class="im-table-scroll">
<table>
	<thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it answers</th></tr></thead>
	<tbody>
		<tr><td><code>brand</code></td><td>The whole look in one answer: the palette in hex for light and dark (hairlines flattened onto the ground), the four faces and what each is for, the logo and its rules, the twelve-column grid, motion in milliseconds <em>and in frames</em> at 30 and 60 fps, the voice, and what it never does.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>brief</code> <small>kind</small></td><td>A recipe: <code>thumbnail</code>, <code>video</code> (titles, lower thirds, cuts, captions, grade, end screen), <code>social</code>, <code>slides</code>, <code>poster</code>, <code>og-image</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>web</code>, <code>app</code>. Canvas sizes, grid, type, colour, timing and a checklist to reject against.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>image_prompt</code> <small>shape, subject</small></td><td>A prompt for an image model that stays in the look — the context block, the shape (thumbnail, short, cover, mark, course, product, portrait, pattern, banner) and what to throw away.</td></tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Ask it plainly: <em>"Use the im-design-system brief for a video and give me the lower-third timings for Premiere at 25 fps"</em>, or <em>"Make a thumbnail for a video about moving to Lisbon, in the Im look"</em>.</p>

<h2>Why both</h2>
<p>The skill is judgement; the server is facts. With only the skill an assistant knows the rules but invents class names. With only the server it can read every file but does not know that a component never sets its own margin, or that <code>{{"{{date}}"}}</code> is a helper. With both it builds a page the way this system would.</p>
